==Evolution==
Evolves into '''Berserker Cat''' at level 10.

Evolves into '''Paladin Cat''' at level 30 using [[Catfruit]] and XP.

==Strategy/Usage==
There are a few factors that hinder this unit's performance before True Form, with its terrible stats, range and somewhat low [[Critical Hit|Critical]] chance, so it's best to invest early to get the most of it. Its highly damaging Critical Hits can quickly put [[:Category:Metal Enemies|Metals]] out of action due to their low health pools crumbling easily when Critical Hits bypass said ability, although that's as far as it goes since its stats are simply not worth using elsewhere.

==='''True Form'''===
Paladin Cat gains an increase in base stats, more range, a higher Critical chance and Immunity to Knockback, heavily improving its overall effectiveness within its niche but not opening up any new doors. This allows him to be a strong anti-Metal attacker, which is compounded by his excellent 2250¢ and sub-50s cooldown, allowing him to easily get value in a single Critical Hit.

However, Paladin Cat faces stringent competition from [[Jurassic Cat (Rare Cat)|Catasaurus]], who has consistent and strong Critical Hits, and can accumulate unmatched Anti-Metal DPS in a stack. Despite this, Paladin Cat remains viable as Catasaurus relies on building a stack to get going, especially against swarms, while Paladin Cat offers immediate and strong Anti-Metal area damage. Paladin Cat even pairs well with Catasaurus; Paladin Cat can burst down a Metal frontline, helping weaken stronger Metals like [[Sir Metal Seal]] and gather cash from weaker Metals that die in one hit, allowing Catasaurus to more easily stack up and get going.

While the [[Immune to Knockback|Knockback Immunity]] does prevent it from being repositioned by [[Super Metal Hippoe]], it isn't a total downgrade either, as said effect will no longer interrupt Paladin's foreswing.

==='''Talents'''===
Paladin's Talents begin with the [[Freeze]] ability, followed by [[Zombie Killer]], [[Immune to Slow|Slow Immunity]], and general stat boosts, but many of them are simply not worth the cost.

At first glance, an [[:Category:Anti-Metal Cats|anti-Metal]] '''Freeze''' effect would've been great to nullify their pushing power, but the activation chance is even lower than the unit's Critical Hits at a measly 30%. Even when maxed with all [[Titanium Fruit]]s, it only goes up to 2.4 seconds, giving it little uptime in practice compared to disposable anti-Metal crowd-control units like [[Clockwork Cat (Rare Cat)|Puppetmaster & Charley]], [[Hoop Cat (Rare Cat)|Hoopmaster Cat]] and Talented [[Panties Cat (Special Cat)|Lollycat]], since they can overwhelm Metals with sheer quantity, giving them a higher overall chance to inflict status effects despite being unable to Crit. Additionally, Paladin's Crits will likely kill most Metals outright, rendering the Freeze pointless.

'''Zombie Killer''' is useless for Paladin Cat, as he is a pure Critter and has no other redeeming qualities; although intended to work on [[March to Death (Merciless)|March to Death]], the only [[:Category:Zombie Enemies|Zombie]] there is [[Daboo of the Dead]], whom Paladin Cat has no hope of killing on his own. Immune to Slow is just as situational, as there are almost no stages where it would even come into play at all.

Level 1 '''Attack Buff''' is actually worth the NP if Paladin Cat himself has also been upgraded to level 50, in which case his Critical damage will increase just enough to knockback the 300% [[Sir Metal Seal]]s found in many late-game stages. Investing any further, however, is only good if you don't have a high-level [[Jurassic Cat (Rare Cat)|Catasaurus]] or any other Critical Hitters that you may use more often than Paladin Cat.

==='''Ultra Talents'''===
Although Paladin Cat gains a few notable Ultra Talents, they pale in comparison to other units for the same Dark Catseye investment, especially since Paladin, being an anti-Metal specialist, boasts terrible stats and is not useful elsewhere.

'''Critical''' boosts the unit's Critical Chance to 60% when maxed. This does improve its consistency, but is far from necessary, as 50% is more than enough to tear down nasty Metals quickly.

'''[[Dodge Attack]]''' against Metal is absolutely useless. 30% to Dodge for 2 seconds isn't too shabby, but the fact that it targets only Metals and how they tend to appear alongside other enemies often limits its scope.

'''[[Money Up]]''' is arguably the best Talent of the three, with how Metal stages tend to be cash-tight and the lack of units that could exploit this ability, but even within this niche, Paladin still faces significant competition to more disposable anti-Metal units, who can function just fine on their own with the right strategy.

